EXTENSION "ARMDef_The_Fallen_Fane_Characters_Saphronia" {
    NAME "Ars Magica Fifth Edition, The Fallen Fane - Saphronia"
    VERSION "0.5.0"
    SPEC_VERSION "0.5"
    RELEASE_DATE "2026-10-09"
    DEPENDS_ON "ARMDef_The_Fallen_Fane_Core_Ext"

    # Saphronia (The Characters, p37): one player character, its sheet the DESCRIPTION.

    #td06b0c7d5d364f3257c0a7 ^"Saphronia" DEF {
        EXTENDS #tHoHTL05ExampleCharacterAA ^"Example Character"
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Role" STRING "Magus"
            ^"House" STRING "Flambeau"
            ^"Covenant" LIST [#tedcb04666f2ea458a379ff ^"Fanum"]
            ^"Votes" INTEGER 1
            ^"Printed Votes" STRING "You only control one vote, and your main aim is to not get executed."
            ^"Character Priority" INTEGER 10
            ^"Printed Introduction" STRING "You are a member of House Flambeau, and of Fanum."
            ^"Source" STRING "The Fallen Fane"
            ^"Page" INTEGER 37
        }
        DESCRIPTION "You are a member of House Flambeau, and of Fanum. You only control one vote, and your main aim is to not get executed.\n\nThe covenant’s plan was not as selfish as many of the other magi believe. The ultimate aim was to drive Christianity from the Rhine Tribunal, and thus reestablish magical auras. Of course, you had to start where you were. The plan started out very subtly, but with increasing success you risked more obvious moves. That was when Doraldus started to get cold feet, and asked around other covenants to see if he could leave. Shortly afterwards, things went wrong, and the crusade was called. Your long work in the local area paid off, and a negotiated settlement was quickly reached, with little loss to either side. There was no way that you could have predicted that other lords, including the one near Schola, would also take up the banner.\n\nNecopinus, the Archmage, was the leader of the covenant, and shaped most of the plans. The younger magi were mostly restricted to following his instructions.\n\nYou were all acting against the Code, and you know it. However, punishment need not fall if you can convince the tribunal that there are other threats they need to face, or that the original aims of the actions were such that they approve of. This might be difficult, but the evidence is far too damning for any other approach to work.\n\nImmediately to the north of the Rhine Tribunal is a region that is completely uncolonized by the Order of Hermes: Scandinavia, the realm of the Order of Odin.\n\nA small group of magi — Arviragus and Saphronia of Fanum, and Coriolanus of Aurora — has been investigating the area, trying to assess the threat to the Order, and the potential for aid and alliances. You have decided to meet at this tribunal, and see what each of you has found.\n\nAfter your journeys to the north, you are fairly sure that there is no Order of Odin. However, you think that it might be useful to invent one. If you can convince the Order that it needs to unite in the face of a powerful and immediate external threat, you might be able to convince them to abandon the idea of harshly punishing your transgressions at Fanum. You will have to convince Arviragus and Coriolanus of the existence of the Order of Odin, and get their backing for your assessment of it.\n\nYou are a Seeker: a magus who is particularly interested in discovering the ancient magical roots of the Order of Hermes. While different Seekers have different interests, they have much in common, particularly in their interest in matters that may have no immediate use. It is quite common for Seekers to meet together and discuss their latest discoveries, although some don’t comment until they’ve completed an investigation, and a few try to keep everything secret.\n\nThe Seekers of the Rhine Tribunal who will be at the meeting are Flavius and Kakondera of Irencillia, Richildis of Aquila, the presiding Quaesitor, Saphronia of Fanum, and Umaldi of Aster.\n\nYour most recent plan was to use the pagan area that Fanum would establish to investigate the relationship between pagan religions and the Old Ones. That obviously fell through, but you might be able to convince your fellow Seekers that it was a worthy goal, and thus its failure does not deserve a heavy punishment."
    }

}
